/target
# Rust build output anywhere (e.g. examples/<pkg>/target from `delulu build`,
# and per-package target/ dirs holding interface.json).
target/
**/*.rs.bk
# Cargo.lock IS tracked (ruling D19c): this workspace ships a binary and a signed release
# artifact, so the exact dependency resolution is part of the build's reproducibility, not a
# library's caller-chosen detail. Do not re-add it here by reflex.

# Toolchain distribution archives, produced by scripts/package-toolchain.sh. Build output, not
# source: the archive is rebuilt from the tree on demand and its checksums come with it.
/dist

# VS Code extension build output. `dist/` is the esbuild bundle, `*.vsix` the package, and
# `node_modules/` the build-time dependencies — all rebuilt by `npm run package` in
# editors/vscode. `package-lock.json` IS tracked: it pins what goes into a published extension.
editors/vscode/node_modules/
editors/vscode/dist/
editors/vscode/*.vsix
# Copied from the repository root by `npm run licence` at package time. Tracked in ONE place only:
# two byte-identical copies drift the first time one is edited, and a stale copy in a published
# extension ships the wrong terms (found by the Survey's `duplicated-file` rule).
editors/vscode/LICENSE

# Built plugin artifacts. `delulu plugin build examples/plugin_shout` writes `shout.dpx` beside the
# package, which is what the example's README tells a reader to do — build output, like `target/`, and
# a .dpx in the tree would become a node in the Survey's map of a CLEAN checkout (the 2026-09-14
# lesson: the packaged .vsix was mapped until a fresh clone counted one node fewer).
*.dpx
